+## When a Night's Sleep Becomes Available
+
+HealthKit has no sleep session object, only stage samples, so Open Wearables builds sessions from them:
+
+1. The app with the SDK uploads new sleep samples when iOS wakes it (HealthKit background delivery or a background refresh). Samples an Apple Watch records usually reach the phone after waking, sometimes hours later: iOS decides when the watch syncs with the phone.
+2. Open Wearables keeps one open session per writer app (the Apple Watch and iPhone count as one, an app such as Oura as another) and closes it once it is complete:
+   - a session with Apple Watch sleep 15 minutes after the last upload that added to it (`SLEEP_UPLOAD_QUIET_MINUTES`), since the watch writes the whole night after waking;
+   - any other session 2 hours after its last sample ended (`SLEEP_END_GAP_MINUTES`), since apps writing to HealthKit can send a night over several syncs;
+   - a night uploaded later than that as soon as it arrives.
+
+   Due sessions are closed every 5 minutes (`SLEEP_SYNC_INTERVAL_SECONDS`). A piece from the same source that arrives after its night was closed is merged into it; one from another source is stored as its own session.
+3. With OW scores on (`OW_SCORES_ENABLED`, the default), the OW sleep score follows within about 10 minutes.
+
+An Apple Watch night is usually available 15 to 20 minutes after it reaches Open Wearables, which can be hours after waking up.
+
 ## Apple Health as a Data Bridge
 
 Even if a provider is **not yet directly supported** by Open Wearables, its data can still be collected by syncing it into Apple Health first.
